Prerequiste
-----------

To build fdkaac, you need the followings:
- libfdk-aac (https://github.com/mstorsjo/fdk-aac)
- CMake, or GNU autoconf/automake

libfdk-aac is always required, and probably you have to build it yourself,
unless you use CMake with -DFDKAAC_BUILD_FDK_AAC=ON (see below), which
fetches and builds it for you.

CMake (recommended)
--------------------

This works the same way on Unix-like systems (Linux, macOS, Cygwin, MinGW,
msys2) and on Windows with MSVC or clang-cl.

1) extract the source, and cd to the source directory
2) cmake -S . -B build
3) cmake --build build -j
4) sudo cmake --install build

By default, libfdk-aac is located via pkg-config, so it (and pkg-config
itself) must be installed first -- e.g. `apt install libfdk-aac-dev` on
Debian/Ubuntu, or `brew install fdk-aac pkg-config` on macOS.

If you don't want to install libfdk-aac yourself (e.g. on Windows, or if
your distro doesn't package it), pass -DFDKAAC_BUILD_FDK_AAC=ON to the
configure step; this fetches libfdk-aac's source and builds it as a static
library, which is linked into fdkaac directly:

    cmake -S . -B build -DFDKAAC_BUILD_FDK_AAC=ON

On Windows, run the above from a "Developer Command Prompt/PowerShell for
Visual Studio" (for MSVC) or any shell with clang-cl in PATH, and add
`-G Ninja` (or another generator of your choice) as needed.

autotools (Unix-like environment)
----------------------------------

Kept around mainly because some distros (e.g. Debian/Ubuntu) package and
build fdkaac this way.

You can build and install libfdk-aac and fdkaac in the following steps.

1) extract the source, and cd to the source directory
2) autoreconf -i
3) ./configure
4) make
5) sudo make install

Depending on your requirements, you might want to add some extra arguments to
configure script.
You have to install libfdk-aac first, and do the same for fdkaac.
